Keep the same world and change as little as the problems require. Fix the problems the check
found, using the suggested fix where it helps, without switching worlds or adding a second
comparison. Keep every cause-and-effect link the analogy already has, in the same order.

Write the spoken version for the ear: two or three sentences, each short enough to say in one
breath, concrete, and ending on the decision. Hear it as the speaker would say it to this
audience. Tighten the wording, but do not drop a cause, an effect, or the choice to make a
sentence shorter.

Keep the mapping list complete, so each player, the constraint, the tradeoff, the cost of doing
nothing, and the decision has its counterpart. Make the breaking point honest and specific: the
one place a sharp listener could push, said plainly, so the speaker can name it first.
